[Music]  welcome to the writing tomatoes lab  critics edition this is the first  installment in a new series of panels  virtual and in person as well as  features and workshops that will focus  on engaging conversations and advice for  aspiring critics  breaking into the industry can be a  complex process and writing tomatoes is  proud to collaborate with experts in the  field to provide guidance and tools for  the next generation of critics  new installments in this series will  publish quarterly on rottentomatoes.com  and be available under the resources tab  i'm sharonda williams producer and host  of the youtube channel pear weight and  our conversation today will focus on the  ins and outs of self-publishing we're so  excited to have the following guests  with us today eric anderson is the  founder and editor-in-chief of awards  watch and a self-professed lover of all  things oscars robert daniels is a  freelance film critic who has written  for roger eber.com the new york times  vulture and polygon he is also the  founder of eight one two film reviews le  lai is the founder and editor-in-chief  of mediaversityreviews a publication  that scores films and tv series on  metrics of diversity kristin lopez is  the tv editor at indy wire as well as a  disabled critic and essayist outside of  indy wire her work has appeared in  variety forbes and mtv so when we get  started i want to ask all of the  panelists a question today  self-publishing plays an important role  in giving aspiring critics a place to  start but there's still a stigma around  reviewing on your own website why should  people persist in creating their own  platform despite industry bias and  robert i'll kick it off to you first  knew you're going to come to me first  [Laughter]  well at least i would say i think one of  the great things about when i first  started self-publishing was the ability  to experiment right you wanna  you're first starting out experiment  with your writing experiment with your  voice um and then also self-publishing  gives you the opportunity to write about  what you wanna write about right if you  start publishing for a larger site then  basically on assignment a lot  but  you know they're if you're into short  films if you're into  more international cinema um whatever it  may be you know  it's a lot easier to try to publish it  on your own  and then go to a site you know a bigger  publication that might not have the  resources or the want to cover it um so  being able to self-publish really just  gives you the chance to  explore what you want to explore whether  it's yourself or the works that you want  to  go over thank you and how about you  i would say uh  rather than in spite of the industry do  it because of the industry itself um if  you're not seeing  the the type of  people that you want to see  doing reviews and doing coverage  you're that you're that person that  if you're looking around for for  somebody else to do it look look at  yourself you're you are that person to  do it  um and and i think that can kick-start  not only your own  uh career but  absolutely inspire people around you  because  it's not it isn't easy to be  a first person you know to do something  if you don't see  uh  the the type of representation that you  want to  to see and you are that person it's  really hard to make that that big first  step  because you know you might not get seen  enough or  you might be worried about  criticism coming to you but if  if if you want it then then you are that  person and you'll inspire other people  without question emily  yeah definitely adding to both what  robert and eric just said it's  definitely a great opportunity to  explore and experiment  and it's absolutely perfect for  for finding gaps that already exist in  criticism and then you know filling  those in yourself  that's definitely  why i even started in the first place my  background's not as a writer so i really  only got into it purely because i felt  like there was something missing and i  and i looked around and i wanted someone  else to do it nobody else was doing it  so  i think that's a great reason to start  your own website is just to be able to  plug the gaps that are in the industry  and kirsten  yeah for me it's a bit of a different  scenario  you know when i was when i was  self-publishing it was more as a outlet  for  what little relaxation i think us as  writers can find uh you know so often  we're assigned things to write about or  are trying to capitalize on the same  things that everybody else is writing  about so for me when i was running my  own website it was because i wanted to  talk about movies that i wasn't being  forced to talk about so i talked about  classic film it was something that i  watched a lot of in my free time and  noticed that a lot of people weren't  talking about it um so so for me it was  kind of a way of getting out of the  critic mindset and just having a fun  outlet to do what i wanted  you

